# Calandria: per-instance configuration.
# Every value is optional; the defaults below are what you get with no env set.
# This one set of variables fully relocates an instance (fresh container, other
# user, other ports) with no code edits. Export them in the environment that
# launches `npm run dev` / `npm start` (server.js reads them before Next boots,
# so a .env file alone is not enough for PORT/CALANDRIA_HOSTNAME/PTY_*).

# ---- deprecated names ----
# Every CALANDRIA_* name below also answers to the ORCH_* spelling this app
# shipped before the rename (a uniform prefix swap, ORCH_FOO -> CALANDRIA_FOO),
# so an existing .env / systemd unit / compose file keeps working untouched. A
# CALANDRIA_* value wins when both are set, and an empty value counts as unset
# on both sides. The server prints one warning line at boot naming whichever
# old names are still in use; that line is your cue to migrate.
# Exceptions: a few names were renamed outright with no old-name fallback
# (CALANDRIA_TASK_ID/_PROJECT_ID/_BASE_URL for the Codex MCP bridge, and the
# test-only CALANDRIA_E2E_*/CALANDRIA_TEST_*), and the docker-compose-only knobs
# (CALANDRIA_USER/PORT/CPUS/MEM/IMAGE/RUNTIME), which compose interpolates
# itself and can't be aliased. Those are a hard rename.

# ---- app server (server.js) ----
# Port and bind address for the single public origin (Next.js + /pty proxy).
#
# Use CALANDRIA_HOSTNAME, not HOSTNAME. The generic name gets set by things
# outside this app (Fedora's /etc/profile exports the machine name to every
# login shell, Docker injects the container id), and reading it made
# `npm start` bind the wrong interface. Bare HOSTNAME is ignored entirely.
#
# Defaults to loopback: a local instance has no login and hands out a shell,
# so binding every interface exposes that to your whole network, and the
# cross-origin gate can't stop a LAN client that forges the Host header.
# Widen it only behind CF_ACCESS_*. The container image sets 0.0.0.0 itself.
PORT=3000
CALANDRIA_HOSTNAME=

# How long, in milliseconds, a SIGTERM/SIGINT (docker stop / Ctrl-C) waits for
# in-flight turns to abort and persist their interrupted state (the same
# settlement a Stop-button press produces) before the process exits anyway.
# Read by both server.js and lib/config.ts; keep them the same value. Default:
# 5000 (5s). That's generous for the local SQLite writes involved, and short
# enough to stay under the container runtime's own SIGKILL grace period.
CALANDRIA_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=

# Background linger: when a Claude turn starts background shell work (Bash
# run_in_background, background subagents), the session's CLI process stays
# alive after the model stops talking. When the work finishes, its completion
# notification wakes the model into a follow-up turn instead of the process
# dying when the turn ends. While lingering, the task shows "working in
# background" (with how long it's been), and new messages queue as usual.
# On by default; set to off/0/false to end turns at result time and let
# background tasks die with the CLI (the pre-feature behavior). Session
# wakeups (ScheduleWakeup / CronCreate / /loop) ride the same linger and only
# fire while the session is held open, so with this off, every pending
# wakeup is reported cancelled in the transcript at the end of the turn.
CALANDRIA_BACKGROUND_LINGER=

# Optional deadline, in milliseconds, on a lingering turn, counted from the
# moment it first starts lingering. Default 0 means no deadline: the session
# waits until the work settles or you press Stop, and the visible "working in
# background · <age>" state tells you when a session is worth kicking. Set a
# positive value to auto-stop lingering work at that age instead; the cut is
# recorded as a transcript notice so the next turn doesn't assume the work
# finished. A scheduled wakeup is waited for only if it falls inside this
# window; one beyond it is cancelled and named in the transcript up front.
CALANDRIA_BACKGROUND_LINGER_MS=

# Tell task sessions to push bulk context collection into subagents: past two
# read-only commands in a row, the third goes to a synchronous Explore/haiku or
# general-purpose/sonnet agent that reports conclusions and file:line rather
# than file contents. Appended to the session prompt for agents that have
# subagents at all (Claude; Codex has no subagent verb and never sees it).
# On by default. It is worded as an override because it is countermanding one:
# the CLI's own auto-mode guidance asks for work to go through Bash and says
# not to call the Agent tool unless the user requested it (docs/DELEGATION.md
# has the measurement). Set to off/0/false to leave sessions on the CLI's
# defaults.
CALANDRIA_DELEGATE_COLLECTION=

# How long a live turn may produce nothing — no assistant text, no tool call,
# no event — before the task card and session header say "no activity for 34m"
# beside the running indicator. Milliseconds; default 1200000 (20 minutes).
# Nothing is stopped or killed: this is a mark, not a deadline, because the
# server cannot tell a wedged wait from a slow one and cutting a real 40-minute
# test run would be worse than the problem. A turn parked on a question or a
# permission card is never marked — that wait is legitimate and open-ended by
# design. Set 0 to turn the mark off.
CALANDRIA_TURN_IDLE_MS=

# How often the idle sweep re-checks live turns. Milliseconds; default 60000,
# clamped to at most CALANDRIA_TURN_IDLE_MS. It runs only while a turn is live.
CALANDRIA_TURN_IDLE_SWEEP_MS=

# Whether a turn marked idle is also TOLD so. Off by default (1/on/true/yes to
# enable) and the only setting here that spends tokens: the session is sent one
# line asking it to re-check whether what it is waiting on is still worth
# waiting for, which starts a real turn. It can only reach a session that is
# lingering — the driver refuses a message mid-thought, so a long build or a
# slow tool call is never interrupted — it is sent at most once per turn, never
# on a scheduled run, and never ahead of a message you already have queued. The
# transcript records that it was sent.
CALANDRIA_TURN_IDLE_NUDGE=

# How every log line is rendered. Empty (the default) or `text` keeps the
# human-readable bracket form this app has always printed, e.g. `[runner]
# turn ok task=abc ms=8412`. `json` emits one JSON object per line instead
# (ts/level/component/msg plus that line's own fields), for an instance whose
# output is shipped to a collector that parses it. Read by server.js,
# pty-server.js and the app alike, so set it once for the whole instance.
# Anything else warns and falls back to text.
CALANDRIA_LOG_FORMAT=

# ---- terminal sidecar (pty-server.js) ----
# The node-pty WebSocket sidecar. Keep it on loopback: the browser never
# connects directly, server.js proxies /pty upgrades to PTY_HOST:PTY_PORT.
PTY_PORT=3001
PTY_HOST=127.0.0.1

# The shell every terminal tab spawns. Empty falls back to $SHELL, then to a
# platform default: on POSIX, the first of /bin/zsh, /bin/bash, /bin/sh that
# exists; on Windows, pwsh.exe/powershell.exe if either is on PATH, else
# %COMSPEC%. $SHELL is a POSIX convention and is unset on native Windows and
# under systemd/trimmed environments, so set this if the drawer can't spawn a
# shell, or to get a different one than your login shell.
# Example: /bin/bash   (Windows: C:\Program Files\PowerShell\7\pwsh.exe)
CALANDRIA_PTY_SHELL=

# ---- public origin ----
# The origin users reach the app on (e.g. https://calandria.example.com behind
# a Cloudflare Tunnel or reverse proxy). The client builds its ws(s):// terminal
# URL from it. Leave empty to use the browser's own origin (window.location),
# which works for any single-hostname deployment, including behind Cloudflare
# Access. Set it if your proxy rewrites the Host header (Cloudflare Tunnel's
# httpHostHeader, for example), since the origin gate then has no other way to
# learn the hostname the browser actually used.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Local mode accepts browser traffic from loopback origins by default
# (localhost, *.localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1). PUBLIC_BASE_URL is also trusted
# automatically. To open a local-mode instance on a LAN or another
# reverse-proxy origin, list exact comma-separated http(s) origins here,
# including non-default ports. Don't use this instead of authentication for
# an internet-exposed instance.
# Example: http://192.168.1.50:3000,https://calandria.internal
CALANDRIA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Lets the pty sidecar accept connections from off-machine. It otherwise
# requires a loopback peer, since server.js proxies to it from the same host,
# a check the caller can't forge, unlike a header. Only use this for a split
# app/sidecar deployment: anything that reaches the sidecar gets a shell.
CALANDRIA_PTY_ALLOW_REMOTE=

# ---- web push (lib/push) ----
# Notifications pushed to subscribed devices (Settings → Notifications) are
# signed with a VAPID key. VAPID_SUBJECT is the contact the browsers' push
# services may use about the traffic: a mailto: or https: URL. Default:
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL when it is https, else mailto:admin@localhost. iOS/iPadOS
# push needs a real subject: Apple rejects mailto:admin@localhost with 403
# BadJwtToken, so set this (or PUBLIC_BASE_URL) to your https origin or a real
# mailto: for iPhone push. Chrome/Android/Firefox accept the default.
VAPID_SUBJECT=

# The VAPID private key, a base64url raw P-256 scalar (the format
# `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys` prints). Leave empty and the instance
# mints one on first use at <CALANDRIA_DB_DIR>/vapid.json. Every subscription
# is bound to the key it was made under, so keep the key with the database.
# Set this only to hold one key across instances sharing a database, or to
# restore subscriptions from a backup onto a fresh data dir.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---- Cloudflare Access (origin-side auth; optional) ----
# If you expose an instance to the internet behind Cloudflare Access, these
# make the origin verify the same identity (the Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion JWT)
# on every route and on the /pty terminal WebSocket, so reaching the port
# directly grants nothing. Enforcement turns on only when both are set; leave
# both empty for local use.
#   CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN: team domain, e.g. your-team.cloudflareaccess.com
#   CF_ACCESS_AUD:         the Access application's aud tag (comma-separable)
CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=
CF_ACCESS_AUD=

# Shared secret that lets health probes (Docker HEALTHCHECK, uptime monitors)
# read the service-token routes, GET /api/version and /api/instance/usage
# only, without an Access JWT, via an `x-service-token: <value>` header.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# It also authenticates two other callers that run inside the box and have no
# Access JWT to present: the boot restore of managed services, and the stdio
# MCP bridge the non-Claude agents' tool calls go through. With CF_ACCESS_*
# set, treat this as required: the container image mints a per-boot token
# when you leave it empty (docker/entrypoint.sh), and a bare-node deployment
# warns at startup. Set it explicitly when something outside the box needs
# to poll.
SERVICE_TOKEN=

# Optional second, fleet-wide read token, accepted on the same two read-only
# routes (usage / version) and nowhere else; it never works on the mutating
# internal agent-tool endpoints, which require the per-instance SERVICE_TOKEN.
# Use it for a metrics dashboard that polls many boxes with one shared secret
# instead of learning each instance's private token. Unset (the default)
# means no such bypass exists. Only useful if you run a fleet.
CALANDRIA_FLEET_TOKEN=

# ---- data locations ----
# SQLite app data (calandria.db) and per-task git worktrees. Both must live
# outside any project repo. Use absolute paths; `~` is not expanded.
# Defaults: $HOME/.calandria and $HOME/.calandria/worktrees.
# If unset, an install that predates the Calandria rename keeps its old
# locations: a $HOME/.zen-orchestrator/orchestrator.db is used where it
# exists, and a populated $HOME/.agent-orchestrator/worktrees is left where it
# is (git records each worktree by absolute path inside its project repo).
# Nothing is moved automatically; boot prints one line naming what it found.
# Inside a directory you set here, calandria.db wins and an existing
# orchestrator.db is the fallback. Migration recipe: docs/SELF_HOSTING.md.
CALANDRIA_DB_DIR=
CALANDRIA_WORKTREES_DIR=

# Where `npm run backup` (scripts/backup.mjs) writes its archives. That script
# takes a WAL-safe hot snapshot with VACUUM INTO, never a file copy of a live
# database, plus uploads, the VAPID key, any persisted API key, and the agent
# CLI login state; worktrees and project clones are reconstructible, so backup
# skips them unless you opt in. The archive holds credentials and is written
# 0600. Put it on a different volume than the one it's backing up.
# Default: <CALANDRIA_DB_DIR>/backups.
CALANDRIA_BACKUP_DIR=

# Run one app process per database. Two processes against one database
# corrupt each other: boot clears what a crash left behind (running flags,
# queued follow-ups, open permission cards, in-flight schedule runs), and
# running that pass against a live instance wipes work in progress. The app
# locks the database at boot and exits with an error if another process
# holds it, naming the holder. Set `off` to skip the check; this is
# unsupported and reintroduces the corruption the check prevents. If you
# want two instances, give them two CALANDRIA_DB_DIRs instead.
# Default: on.
CALANDRIA_DB_LOCK=
# How long boot retries the lock before giving up. The wait covers a
# predecessor still shutting down; a crashed one releases instantly.
# Default: 10000.
CALANDRIA_DB_LOCK_WAIT_MS=

# Where "New project → Clone from GitHub" puts cloned repos. Absolute path.
# Default: $HOME/projects.
CALANDRIA_PROJECTS_DIR=

# Largest single chat attachment, in megabytes. Any file type may be attached —
# a screenshot, a log bundle, a spreadsheet, a PDF, an archive. The file is
# staged under <CALANDRIA_DB_DIR>/uploads/<task>/, deliberately outside the
# task's git worktree, and only its PATH goes into the message: the bytes never
# enter the model's context unless the agent decides to open the file. So this
# cap is about disk and about the server's heap (a multipart body is buffered
# whole while it is parsed), not about the context window. Staged files are
# removed when the task is deleted, when its transcript ages out, and when the
# worktree sweep reclaims its checkout.
# Default: 25.
CALANDRIA_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=

# ---- git remotes ----
# The app fetches a project's base branch, best-effort, when the project is
# opened and again before cutting a new task worktree, so tasks start from
# the real tip rather than a local `main` that went stale the moment a PR
# merged on GitHub. It never pulls and never moves your local branch on its
# own, and it never blocks a task launch; the worst case is a slower start
# and the same purely local behavior as before. Set to off/0/false for an
# air-gapped instance or a repo whose fetch is too expensive to run routinely.
# Default: on.
CALANDRIA_GIT_FETCH=

# Hard deadline for that fetch, in ms. The subprocess is killed at this point
# and the launch continues from the best local ref. Default: 10000.
CALANDRIA_GIT_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=

# How long a successful fetch counts as fresh, in ms. Opening a project and
# immediately launching several tasks costs one fetch, not one each.
# Default: 15000.
CALANDRIA_GIT_FETCH_COOLDOWN_MS=

# ---- managed services ----
# Base of the deterministic per-project port block. Each project is assigned
# base + slot at creation (stored on projects.port), and that PORT is
# injected into its supervised dev/setup/test services and its PTY shell.
# Relocate to avoid clashing with the app/sidecar ports. Default: 4300.
CALANDRIA_SERVICE_PORT_BASE=

# Per-service in-memory log ring buffer, in lines. Bounds what the supervisor
# keeps of each service's stdout/stderr for the Services drawer.
# Default: 1500.
CALANDRIA_SERVICE_LOG_LINES=

# Public service hostnames (<slug>--<appHost>, e.g.
# calc--calandria.example.com): set to 1 to let server.js reverse-proxy
# service hostnames to the local ports, with per-service visibility
# (private / shared link / public). Off by default and kept separate from
# CALANDRIA_FEATURE_SERVICES, so enabling managed services never exposes
# anything publicly on its own. Requires PUBLIC_BASE_URL (the instance's own
# hostname is derived from it) plus wildcard DNS/TLS covering *--<that host>;
# without both, services keep their plain http://localhost:<port> URLs.
CALANDRIA_SERVICE_HOSTS=

# ---- claude binary ----
# Path to the logged-in `claude` CLI (Pro/Max). Set explicitly because Next's
# server may run with a trimmed PATH. Default: $HOME/.local/bin/claude.
#
# On Windows the default is %USERPROFILE%\.local\bin\claude.exe (where the
# native installer puts it), falling back to whatever `claude` resolves to on
# PATH. The extension matters: Node spawns without a shell, so a name with no
# extension can't run. Point this at a real `claude.exe` rather than an npm
# `claude.cmd` shim: the Agent SDK spawns this path directly, and Node can't
# launch a .cmd that way.
#
# Windows prerequisite: Git for Windows must be installed and on PATH. Claude
# Code runs its Bash tool through Git Bash even when launched from PowerShell,
# so without it every Bash tool call in every turn fails, and Calandria's own
# worktree/diff/merge machinery also shells out to `git`.
CLAUDE_CLI_PATH=

# ---- codex binary ----
# Path to the `codex` CLI the Codex agent driver drives (ChatGPT-plan login).
# Empty = the SDK auto-resolves the binary bundled with @openai/codex, and the
# auth helpers fall back to `codex` on PATH (installed globally in the image).
# Set to pin a specific binary when PATH is trimmed. Default: (auto-resolve).
# On Windows the PATH lookup applies PATHEXT, so a global npm install's
# `codex.cmd` shim is found and launched through cmd.exe.
CODEX_CLI_PATH=

# ---- agy binary ----
# Path to the Antigravity CLI (`agy`), the binary behind the Gemini agent
# driver. Empty = resolve bare `agy` on PATH (installed in the image next to
# `claude` and `codex`). Set to pin a specific binary when PATH is trimmed.
# Default: (auto-resolve).
AGY_CLI_PATH=

# ---- gh binary ----
# Path to the GitHub CLI (`gh`) used for repo listing/cloning, the guided
# device-flow login, and opening PRs. Empty means auto-resolve: bare `gh` if
# the server's PATH can see it, else a best-effort probe of the usual install
# dirs (/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin, /usr/local/bin,
# /snap/bin, ~/.local/bin; on Windows %LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\WinGet\Links,
# %ProgramFiles%\GitHub CLI and ~\scoop\shims). The server process never reads
# a shell profile, so a gh that works in your terminal (Homebrew, snap, scoop)
# can be invisible to the app's PATH. Set this to its full path if the probe
# misses yours. Default: (auto-resolve).
CALANDRIA_GH_BIN=

# ---- pull-request state ----
# Once a task has a PR, the server keeps its state (open/merged/closed, the
# check rollup, the review decision) fresh from `gh pr view` and publishes the
# changes over /api/events. Three knobs bound what that costs.
#
# How long a PR snapshot counts as fresh, in ms. Opening a task, its Refresh
# button and the create-PR trigger all skip gh entirely inside this window.
# Default: 60000.
CALANDRIA_PR_STALE_MS=

# How often the background sweep re-reads OPEN PRs, in ms. 0 disables the sweep
# and leaves the on-open and explicit-Refresh triggers. The sweep stops itself
# when no task has an open PR, and skips a pass when no browser tab is watching.
# Default: 300000 (5 minutes).
CALANDRIA_PR_POLL_MS=

# Most PRs refreshed per sweep (one `gh pr view` subprocess each, oldest first).
# Default: 5.
CALANDRIA_PR_POLL_BATCH=

# How many lines of a failed job's log the "Fix CI" button seeds its turn with,
# per failing check. `gh run view --log-failed` already drops the green steps,
# but only the END of a failing suite says what broke, so this is a tail. Raise
# it for CI that prints long stack traces; lower it for lint-only pipelines.
# Default: 200.
CALANDRIA_CI_LOG_TAIL_LINES=

# ---- tool permissions (Claude, "Accept edits" / "Plan mode") ----
# How long a permission prompt parks waiting for a decision while at least
# one browser tab is watching, in milliseconds. Generous by default so nobody
# hits it mid-review, but bounded so a prompt left overnight releases the
# turn instead of holding the task "running" (and the instance busy) forever.
# 0 parks indefinitely, like an unanswered question card.
# Default: 14400000 (4h).
CALANDRIA_PERMISSION_PROMPT_TIMEOUT_MS=

# The same deadline when nobody is watching: an auto-started task, or any
# turn running while the app is closed. Nobody can answer, so the prompt
# fails closed quickly rather than wedging the turn; the window exists so a
# tab opening moments later still gets to decide. 0 always uses the attended
# timeout above. Default: 45000 (45s).
CALANDRIA_PERMISSION_UNATTENDED_MS=

# The `approval_policy` the Codex driver passes to the CLI. Default "never"
# (unattended auto-run in isolated worktrees). Enterprise-managed Codex
# deployments can disallow "never"; Calandria detects the CLI's downgrade
# warning and switches to "on-request" from the next turn on, so usually
# nothing needs to be set here. To pick a policy up front, use "on-request"
# or "on-failure", or "inherit" to omit the override so ~/.codex/config.toml
# and the managed requirements decide. Do not use "untrusted"/UnlessTrusted:
# Calandria's non-interactive sessions can't answer its approval prompts, so
# every command is rejected (it maps to "on-request").
CODEX_APPROVAL_POLICY=

# Whether Codex tasks also mount the MCP servers from your
# ~/.codex/config.toml, alongside Calandria's own bridge. Default off, unlike
# Claude tasks (which do inherit ~/.claude MCP servers): `codex exec` has no
# approver, so an inherited server's tools are visible to the model but every
# call comes back as "user cancelled MCP tool call". The driver unmounts them
# instead of leaving tools that can't work. Set to 1 to mount them anyway,
# for example if you've set default_tools_approval_mode = "approve" on your
# own servers.
CODEX_INHERIT_MCP=

# ---- internal agent-tool bridge ----
# The loopback origin the stdio MCP bridge (scripts/calandria-mcp.mjs, spawned
# by the Codex CLI per turn) uses to POST suggest_task / expose_service back
# to the app's internal endpoints. Authenticated with SERVICE_TOKEN. Empty
# means http://127.0.0.1 on the app's own PORT, correct for the in-container
# default. Override only if the app is reached differently from inside the box.
CALANDRIA_INTERNAL_BASE_URL=

# ---- local models ----
# Where a project's "Model provider → Local model" preset (Settings → project)
# and the `suggest_task` tool's `provider: "local"` point by default: an Ollama
# or LM Studio server, or anything serving both the Anthropic Messages API
# (Claude Code, via ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL) and the OpenAI Responses API (Codex,
# via a provider entry Calandria writes). Only the DEFAULT is instance-wide —
# the preset copies the URL into the project, where it can be edited. A Docker
# instance reaches the host's Ollama at http://host.docker.internal:11434.
# Recipes: docs/AGENTS.md "Local models". Default: http://localhost:11434
CALANDRIA_LOCAL_MODEL_BASE_URL=

# ---- agent credentials (usually not set here) ----
# The recommended path is subscription login done in the app:
#   - Claude: onboarding / Settings "Connect Claude"   → login state in ~/.claude
#   - Codex:  Settings → Agents "Connect Codex" (device auth) → ~/.codex
# Both credential dirs live on the per-user home volume, so logins survive
# restarts with no env vars.
#
# The "I have an API key instead" paths persist their key to a 0600 file under
# CALANDRIA_DB_DIR (anthropic-api-key / openai-api-key) and re-apply it to the
# process env on boot; you normally don't set these here.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN / OPENAI_API_KEY found in the
# launch environment are stripped at boot (lib/env-keys.mjs +
# docker/entrypoint.sh, with a warning in the log). A key inherited from a
# shell profile, environment.d, or a systemd unit would otherwise silently
# bill every turn per-token while the UI reports your subscription login. To
# seed a key from the environment anyway, for a headless/dev instance, opt in:
#   CALANDRIA_ALLOW_API_KEY_ENV=1   keep env-provided keys; turns bill them
#                                   per-token (the UI will report API-key billing)
CALANDRIA_ALLOW_API_KEY_ENV=

# ---- per-instance resource caps (docker-compose.yml) ----
# What docker compose passes to the container runtime for THIS instance
# (--cpus / --memory). Defaults: 2 CPUs, 4g memory.
CALANDRIA_CPUS=
CALANDRIA_MEM=

# ---- image (docker-compose.yml) ----
# Which image the compose service runs. Empty means calandria:latest, what
# `docker build -t calandria .` produces from this checkout. Point it at the
# published multi-arch build to skip building entirely (see
# docs/SELF_HOSTING.md):
#   CALANDRIA_IMAGE=ghcr.io/calandria-dev/calandria:latest
CALANDRIA_IMAGE=

# ---- feature flags ----
# Unset/empty keeps each flag's shipped default; an explicit value decides.
# In-progress flags default off; shipped flags default on.

# PREVIEW tab (project live-URL view). Off until the remote-execution backend
# that actually runs and hosts the app to serve a real URL is in place. Set
# to 1 to surface the tab in the session rail. Default: off.
CALANDRIA_FEATURE_LIVE_PREVIEW=

# Command palette: the toolbar "Jump to project, session, or command…" search
# bar and its ⌘K / Ctrl-K shortcut. Fuzzy-jumps to any project or session and
# runs common commands. Set to 1 to enable. Default: off.
CALANDRIA_FEATURE_OMNI_SEARCH=

# Test-only: registers the deterministic mock agent driver (lib/agents/mock/)
# in the agent registry. Set automatically by the Playwright e2e suite (see
# e2e/README.md) so turns run without a real agent CLI or login. Never set
# this on a real instance; the mock would appear in the agent picker.
# Default: off.
CALANDRIA_E2E_MOCK_AGENT=

# Experimental: registers the Gemini agent driver (lib/agents/gemini/), which
# drives Google's Antigravity CLI (`agy`). Off by default while the driver is
# being proven out — with it unset, the agent never appears in the picker and
# no `agy` binary is required. Set to 1 to try it; you also need to connect the
# agent (Settings → Agents), which signs in with your Google account.
#
# In a CONTAINER, sign-in is the catch: `agy` keeps its OAuth tokens in the
# D-Bus Secret Service and the published image ships no D-Bus, so the
# subscription login cannot be completed there. Set GEMINI_API_KEY instead and
# the driver configures the CLI to use it — that path bills Google's API rather
# than drawing on a Google AI subscription. On a normal desktop install (where a
# keyring is running) the subscription login works as usual.
# Default: off.
CALANDRIA_EXPERIMENTAL_GEMINI=

# Managed Services: the toolbar "Services" button/drawer, the Services
# command config (dev/setup/test) block in the project-context editor, and
# the persisted supervisor. Registry rows survive restarts, managed dev
# servers auto-restart on boot (reaping orphaned process groups from a
# crashed server first), and port conflicts surface as a readable error
# instead of a crash loop. Default: on; set to 0 to disable. Public service
# hostnames are not included: they stay opt-in via CALANDRIA_SERVICE_HOSTS
# above, so this flag alone exposes nothing publicly.
CALANDRIA_FEATURE_SERVICES=

# ---- scheduled tasks (lib/scheduler.ts) ----
# Master switch for the schedule ticker. On by default; off/0/false stops
# this instance from ever starting scheduled work on its own. Default: on.
CALANDRIA_SCHEDULER=

# How often the ticker wakes to check for due firings, in ms. Default: 30000.
CALANDRIA_SCHEDULE_TICK_MS=

# How late a missed firing may still run, in ms. Past this it's recorded as
# `missed` instead, never skipped silently. 0 disables catch-up.
# Default: 14400000 (4h).
CALANDRIA_SCHEDULE_CATCHUP_MS=

# How long the fire-time slash-command check may take before the sweep gives
# up on it, in ms. It spawns the agent CLI inside the ticker's single-flight
# sweep, so an unbounded check would wedge every schedule on the instance.
# Default: 20000.
CALANDRIA_SCHEDULE_PROBE_MS=

# ---- retention (lib/retention.ts) ----
# Everything except the schedule run ledger used to grow forever: rows left
# only by cascade when you manually deleted the owning task or project. A
# sweep rides the schedule ticker (same process, no second daemon) and ages
# out the record of tasks that are finished: terminal (done/cancelled), idle,
# not snoozed, nothing queued behind them. A live task is never touched.
#
# Master switch. On by default; off/0/false/no keeps everything forever,
# which is the pre-retention behavior and the right setting if your
# transcripts are a record somebody audits. Default: on.
CALANDRIA_RETENTION=

# How long a finished task keeps its own record: transcript, review
# comments, the sessions a /clear retired, and its uploaded attachments.
# Days. 0 keeps them forever. Default: 180.
CALANDRIA_RETENTION_DAYS=

# How long the spend rows live (task_usage, task_merges, internal_usage).
# Days. Set longer than the window above: these feed Insights, which reads
# 180 days back and asks for the same width again for prior-period deltas,
# so a shorter value would carve a hole in a chart on screen. 0 keeps them
# forever. Default: 400.
CALANDRIA_USAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=

# How often the sweep runs, in ms. Not the ticker's 30s interval; a policy
# measured in months doesn't need re-scanning every half minute. Also paces
# the worktree sweep and the disk-usage check below, the same kind of policy
# on the same ticker. Default: 21600000 (6h).
CALANDRIA_RETENTION_SWEEP_MS=

# Run a full VACUUM after a sweep that deleted something. Off by default.
# The sweep always checkpoints the WAL (TRUNCATE), which reclaims the file
# that grows during a big delete; only VACUUM shrinks calandria.db itself,
# and it rewrites the whole database under a write lock. Set to 1/on/true to
# opt in.
CALANDRIA_RETENTION_VACUUM=

# ---- worktree retention + disk warning (lib/worktreeSweep.ts) ----
# Per-task git worktrees are each a full checkout of the project repo, so
# they're the biggest disk-growth vector here and the only one measured in
# gigabytes. Reclaiming one is safe: the next turn re-cuts it from the task's
# branch. The sweep only touches finished tasks (the same terminal/idle/cold
# predicate as above) and always skips a checkout holding uncommitted edits
# or unmerged commits, however old. Branches are never deleted by the sweep,
# only the checkout goes.
#
# Opt-in, unlike the table prune: those windows (180/400 days) are longer
# than most instances have existed, but a worktree window measured in weeks
# would start removing checkouts on the first tick after an upgrade nobody
# asked for. Set to 1/on/true/yes. Default: off.
CALANDRIA_WORKTREE_RETENTION=

# How long a finished task keeps its worktree once the sweep is on. Days.
# 0 keeps them forever. Default: 14.
CALANDRIA_WORKTREE_RETENTION_DAYS=

# Warn in the server log when the worktrees directory crosses this size, in
# GB. Runs whether or not the sweep above is enabled, since an instance that
# hasn't opted in is the one that most needs telling; the remedy is a human
# in Settings -> Storage, which shows the same number. 0 disables it.
# Default: 20.
CALANDRIA_WORKTREES_DISK_WARN_GB=

# ---- metrics (lib/metrics.ts) ----
# GET /api/instance/metrics serves Prometheus text exposition: turn
# counters, live turns, DB and worktree footprint, schedule-run outcomes. It
# runs unconditionally and is gated exactly like /api/version: under
# Cloudflare Access a scraper presents SERVICE_TOKEN or CALANDRIA_FLEET_TOKEN
# in x-service-token; in local mode a loopback scrape needs nothing. See
# docs/SELF_HOSTING.md.
#
# How long one measurement of the worktrees directory is reused between
# scrapes, in ms. Everything else on that endpoint is cheap; this one is a
# `du` over every task checkout on the box, so a 15s scrape interval would
# otherwise walk every node_modules on the instance four times a minute.
# Raise it if you carry many large worktrees; 0 measures on every scrape.
# Default: 60000 (1m).
CALANDRIA_METRICS_SIZE_TTL_MS=

# ---- subscription plan usage (lib/agents/claude/planUsage.ts) ----
# The titlebar session/week usage meter for a Claude Pro/Max login.
# Percentages come from the same OAuth usage endpoint the Claude CLI's
# /usage panel reads (cached instance-wide, fetched only while a tab is
# polling), overlaid with the free rate-limit telemetry every turn already
# carries. Off/0/false hides the meter and guarantees the app never calls
# the usage endpoint. Default: on.
CALANDRIA_PLAN_USAGE=

# Floor between two fetches of the provider usage endpoint, in ms. Anthropic
# rate-limits it aggressively; 300000 (5 min) matches the Claude CLI's own
# minimum interval for the same endpoint. Default: 300000.
CALANDRIA_PLAN_USAGE_MIN_FETCH_MS=

# ---- desktop shell auto-update (desktop/updater.js) ----
# Read by the Electron desktop app only, and read off process.env directly:
# desktop/ has no lib/config.ts and deliberately does not load one, so this is
# documented here rather than declared there. The server ignores it entirely.
#
# On (the default) means CHECK and DOWNLOAD, never install. An available update
# is announced as an OS notification plus a tray/menu item, and the restart only
# happens when the user asks for it — at which point it goes through the same
# quit that drains in-flight turns, never around it. Off/0/false/no stops the
# shell from contacting the update feed at all. Default: on.
#
# Two installs ignore this because they cannot self-update either way: a
# development build (`cd desktop && npm start`), and a Linux install that is not
# an AppImage — a .deb belongs to your package manager, and letting
# electron-updater have it would mean an unverified `sudo dpkg -i`. Both say so
# in the menu instead of failing oddly. macOS additionally needs a real
# Developer ID signature: Squirrel.Mac will not install into an unsigned or
# ad-hoc build. See docs/DESKTOP_APP.md §6.6.
CALANDRIA_DESKTOP_AUTO_UPDATE=

# ---- desktop shell launch environment (desktop/env-file.js) ----
# Read by the Electron desktop app only; the server ignores both of these.
#
# The desktop app has no wrapper script in front of it -- a Finder/Dock/Login
# Item launch hands main.js launchd's own minimal environment, with nothing
# sourced and nothing exported. CALANDRIA_ENV_FILE names a plain KEY=VALUE file
# the supervisor reads before spawning either sidecar, as the desktop
# replacement for the launcher self-hosted deployments are expected to write
# (docs/SELF_HOSTING.md, "Configuration"). Empty means the default,
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/calandria/env or ~/.config/calandria/env if that's unset --
# the same path on every platform on purpose. A missing file is not an error;
# it just means nothing is layered in. See docs/DESKTOP_APP.md.
CALANDRIA_ENV_FILE=

# Whether the desktop shell probes the login shell for a better PATH when the
# inherited one looks like launchd's GUI stub (/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin
# and nothing else). `auto` (default) probes only in that case; `always` probes
# on every launch (costs a real shell startup -- rc files, version managers);
# `off` never probes. Skipped automatically when CALANDRIA_ENV_FILE sets PATH
# itself. See docs/DESKTOP_APP.md.
CALANDRIA_DESKTOP_PATH_PROBE=
